  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Record warmth possible today.\n\nMild, potentially record breaking (see climate section) and dry \ntoday ahead of an incoming cold front. Winds will pick up today as a \nstrengthening low-level jet of 35 to 45 knot moves over the eastern \nGreat Lakes. That said...poor low level lapse rates will prevent \nfull mixing of stronger winds from aloft, but channeling down Lake \nErie may still support gusts of 30-40 mph across the Niagara \nFrontier in the afternoon. Gusts of 20 to 30 mph are expected \nelsewhere. Otherwise...another mild day with Max T's peaking in the \nlow to mid 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chance of showers tonight. Dry weather then \nresumes Friday and lasts through the weekend. \n\nA weak cold front will begin to cross the eastern Great Lakes\nlate this evening and tonight. Decent forcing and moisture \nconvergence will likely support some showers and maybe even a \nrumble of thunder as guidance shows MUCAPE values of 150-200 \nJ/kg. Precipitation wise, amounts look very meager with most \nspots only picking up a few hundredths of an inch, with localize\nhigher amounts. Showers will exit before daybreak Friday, with \nhigh pressure then building back into the region later Friday \nthrough the weekend with another extended stretch of dry \nweather.",
